WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.978
A headline is a clue, not a job offer.

2
00:00:02.978 --> 00:00:06.694
Start with the question you are actually trying to answer.

3
00:00:06.694 --> 00:00:11.343
Sam wants an entry-level job within travelling distance of home.

4
00:00:11.343 --> 00:00:15.962
Document A reports a provincial median of twenty-eight dollars an hour.

5
00:00:15.962 --> 00:00:19.468
Median means the middle observation in an ordered set.

6
00:00:19.468 --> 00:00:22.712
It does not mean every worker earns that amount.

7
00:00:22.712 --> 00:00:28.146
The data include different experience levels, so Sam cannot treat the median as

8
00:00:28.146 --> 00:00:29.400
a starting wage.

9
00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:34.192
Document B lists a local assistant job at nineteen dollars an hour.

10
00:00:34.192 --> 00:00:39.460
That is useful for this particular opening, but it does not describe the

11
00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:40.270
entire occupation.

12
00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:44.359
It may also be a different role from the occupation in Document A.

13
00:00:44.359 --> 00:00:46.179
Now compare the dates.

14
00:00:46.179 --> 00:00:53.795
The report was published in 2026, but its wage observations are from 2024.

15
00:00:53.795 --> 00:00:58.685
A recent publication date does not automatically make every number current.

16
00:00:58.685 --> 00:01:04.378
A careful conclusion is: I have evidence worth investigating, but not a guaranteed

17
00:01:04.378 --> 00:01:06.130
job or starting salary.

18
00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:12.084
Sam's next step is to check the role, entry requirements and travel, then

19
00:01:12.084 --> 00:01:13.916
compare another relevant source.

